---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PaceCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/PaceCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pace-calculator">
  <PaceCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the pace calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Choose what you want to solve for: pace from a distance and time, a finish time from a target
        pace, or how far you covered at a known pace.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pick a standard distance or enter your own. Metric and imperial paces are shown side by side
        either way.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fill in the other two values. The results update as you type, so there is no calculate button.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the split table to plan a race, and the equivalent-times table to see what the performance
        predicts at other distances.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Pace, speed and how they relate</h2>
    <p>
      Pace is time per unit of distance, and speed is distance per unit of time. They are reciprocals.
      Runners think in pace, because you can hold a target pace.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pace (min/km) = total time in minutes ÷ distance in km</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Pace (min/mile) = pace per km × 1.609344</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Speed (km/h) = 60 ÷ pace in min/km</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Speed (mph) = 60 ÷ pace in min/mile</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A quick mental conversion: to go from min/km to min/mile, add about 60 percent. 5:00/km becomes
      roughly 8:00/mile (exactly 8:03). 6:00/km becomes about 9:39/mile.
    </p>

    <h2>Common pace to speed conversions</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Pace / km</th>
            <th scope="col">Pace / mile</th>
            <th scope="col">km/h</th>
            <th scope="col">mph</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>3:00</td><td>4:50</td><td>20.0</td><td>12.4</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3:30</td><td>5:38</td><td>17.1</td><td>10.6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4:00</td><td>6:26</td><td>15.0</td><td>9.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4:30</td><td>7:15</td><td>13.3</td><td>8.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5:00</td><td>8:03</td><td>12.0</td><td>7.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5:30</td><td>8:51</td><td>10.9</td><td>6.8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6:00</td><td>9:39</td><td>10.0</td><td>6.2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6:30</td><td>10:28</td><td>9.2</td><td>5.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7:00</td><td>11:16</td><td>8.6</td><td>5.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8:00</td><td>12:52</td><td>7.5</td><td>4.7</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Target paces for common goal times</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Goal</th>
            <th scope="col">Distance</th>
            <th scope="col">Pace / km</th>
            <th scope="col">Pace / mile</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sub-20 5K</td><td>5 km</td><td>3:59</td><td>6:26</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-25 5K</td><td>5 km</td><td>4:59</td><td>8:02</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-30 5K</td><td>5 km</td><td>5:59</td><td>9:39</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-45 10K</td><td>10 km</td><td>4:29</td><td>7:14</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-50 10K</td><td>10 km</td><td>4:59</td><td>8:02</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-2:00 half</td><td>21.0975 km</td><td>5:41</td><td>9:09</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-1:45 half</td><td>21.0975 km</td><td>4:58</td><td>8:00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-1:30 half</td><td>21.0975 km</td><td>4:16</td><td>6:52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-4:00 marathon</td><td>42.195 km</td><td>5:41</td><td>9:09</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-3:30 marathon</td><td>42.195 km</td><td>4:58</td><td>8:00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub-3:00 marathon</td><td>42.195 km</td><td>4:16</td><td>6:52</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The pace for a sub-two-hour half marathon and a sub-four-hour marathon is the same: 5:41/km.
      Sub-1:45 half and sub-3:30 marathon both need 4:58/km. Double the distance at the same pace and
      the time exactly doubles, which is much harder than it looks.
    </p>

    <h2>Predicting race times across distances</h2>
    <p>
      Pete Riegel's 1977 formula is the standard method:
    </p>
    <p><strong>T₂ = T₁ × (D₂ ÷ D₁)<sup>1.06</sup></strong></p>
    <p>
      The exponent of 1.06 makes it realistic. At 1.00, double the distance would double the time. At
      1.06, double the distance costs about 4.2 percent more than double the time, because pace must
      drop as distance grows.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works best when the two distances are within a factor of about four, and when you trained for
      the longer one. A marathon predicted from a 5K on 5K mileage is much too fast: marathon
      performance depends on fuelling, durability and long-run volume that a 5K cannot measure. A 10K
      predicted from a half marathon is usually accurate to within a minute.
    </p>

    <h2>Even splits, negative splits and the wall</h2>
    <p>
      The split table on this page assumes even pacing. That is the correct default: almost all
      distance world records were set with even or slightly negative splits, and a start that is too
      fast is the most common reason for a bad race.
    </p>
    <p>
      A negative split means the second half is faster than the first. In practice, run the first two
      or three kilometres 5 to 10 seconds per kilometre slower than target, then hold target pace, and
      use what is left in the last quarter. It feels too slow at the start, but it almost always gives a
      faster finish time.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the marathon, "the wall" at about 30–32 km is mostly glycogen depletion. Even pacing plus 30 to
      60 g of carbohydrate per hour from the start moves it much later, and often removes it.
    </p>

    <h2>What moves your real pace</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hills: about 12–20 seconds per kilometre slower per 1 percent of uphill gradient, and about half that back on the way down. Rolling courses are always slower than flat ones.</li>
      <li>Heat: above about 15 °C, expect to lose 2 to 5 percent on marathon pace, more if humidity is high.</li>
      <li>Wind: a headwind costs much more than the same tailwind gives back. Run behind someone if you can.</li>
      <li>Altitude: above 1,500 m, endurance pace drops noticeably until you acclimatise, which takes two to three weeks.</li>
      <li>Surface: trail and soft ground can cost 30 to 60 seconds per kilometre against tarmac at the same effort.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So pace is a plan, not a promise. Run the first half on effort, compare it with the split table,
      and adjust.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
